.TH KD 1 "2026-08-01" "kd 0.1.1" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
kd \- list directory contents
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B kd
.RI [ options ]
.RI [ path .\|.\|.]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B kd
lists directory contents in columns, or in a long format with metadata.
It is written in plain C and depends on nothing beyond libc.
.PP
With no
.I path
argument the current directory is listed.
.PP
Entries
.B .
and
.B ..
are never listed.
.SH OPTIONS
Short options may be clustered, so
.B \-laG
is equivalent to
.B \-l \-a \-G .
.TP
.B \-l
Long format: permissions, link count, owner, size, timestamp, name. The
listing opens with a header line and closes with a summary row.
.IP
The permission field is nine characters. The leading type character of
.BR ls (1)
is not printed, so the listing lines up with the header. Directories and
symlinks are distinguished by name color, and a symlink also shows its
target.
.TP
.BR \-a ", " \-A
Include entries whose names begin with a dot.
.TP
.BR \-B ", " \-\-bytes
Print sizes as raw byte counts instead of readable units.
.TP
.B \-D
List directories only.
.TP
.BR \-g ", " \-\-group
Show the group column. Off by default, as it usually repeats the owner.
.TP
.BR \-G ", " \-\-git
Begin each line with the subject of the last commit that touched the entry,
the way a github directory listing reads, green where the entry has changed
since. Implies
.BR \-\-short .
Long format only. See
.B GIT
below.
.TP
.BR \-s ", " \-S ", " \-\-short
Long format without permissions, link count and owner: size, timestamp and
name only. Implies
.BR \-l .
.TP
.B \-m
Page the output. See
.B PAGER
below.
.TP
.BR \-W ", " \-\-where
Print full paths instead of bare names.
.TP
.BR \-T ", " \-\-tree
Recursive tree view.
.TP
.B \-1
One entry per line.
.TP
.B \-h
Accepted and ignored. Readable sizes are already the default.
.TP
.BI \-\-color= when
Colorize output.
.I when
is
.BR auto ", " always ", or " never .
The
.BR ls (1)
synonyms
.BR tty ", " if\-tty ", " yes ", " force ", " no " and " none
are accepted as well.
Default is
.BR auto ,
which colorizes only when standard output is a terminal.
.TP
.B \-\-group\-directories\-first
Sort directories before other entries.
.TP
.BR \-\-ls\-colors ", " \-\-no\-ls\-colors
Take file name colors from the
.B LS_COLORS
environment variable, or do not. Off by default.
.TP
.BR \-\-ext\-colors ", " \-\-no\-ext\-colors
Color file names by extension, or do not. On by default.
.TP
.BR \-\-header ", " \-\-no\-header
Print the header line above a long listing, or do not. On by default.
See
.B HEADER
below.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-pager
Never page automatically.
.PP
.BR \-\-icons ", " \-\-no\-icons " and " \-\-sort
are accepted and ignored, so that aliases written for
.BR eza (1)
keep working.
.SH SIZES
Sizes are decimal and roll up at 1000, except kilobytes, which hold until
10000. A 1.5 MB file reads as
.BR 1500K ,
500 MB as
.BR 500M ,
and 1.2 GB as
.BR 1.2G .
A decimal is shown only when the mantissa is below 100.
.SH HEADER
Long format opens with a header line: the absolute path of the directory
being listed, as
.BR pwd (1)
would report it, followed by the origin remote and the current branch when
the directory is inside a git work tree. The three are joined by colons and
set apart by color. The path is abbreviated to
.B ~
under the home directory.
.PP
.EX
~/kd:github.com/user/kd:main
.EE
.PP
The remote is reduced to host, owner and repository, so both
.B git@github.com:user/repo.git
and
.B https://github.com/user/repo.git
show as
.BR github.com/user/repo .
With a detached
.B HEAD
the branch is replaced by the short commit hash, marked with a leading
.BR @ .
.PP
The line is fitted to the terminal width by giving up the least useful part
first: the host is dropped from the remote, then the remote entirely, then
the branch. The path is never shortened. Determining the branch and remote
runs
.BR git (1)
once or twice per listing; use
.B \-\-no\-header
to avoid that, or to keep the output of
.B \-l
purely tabular.
.SH GIT
.B \-G
opens every line of a long listing with the subject of the last commit that
touched the entry, and drops permissions, link count and owner to pay for it.
.PP
.EX
open long listings with a header line     8.6K     1 hour ago kd.1
directory lister in plain C               101K   19 hours ago kd2.png
                                            18       just now new.txt
.EE
.PP
The subject is cut with an ellipsis where it does not fit. A leading
.I reponame:
is stripped, the repo already being named in the header. A directory carries
the subject of the last commit touching anything beneath it, and an entry that
has never been committed leaves the field blank.
.PP
Status is carried by color rather than by a column of its own. An entry that
.B "git status"
reports as changed has its subject painted
.BR KF_GIT_DIRTY ,
everything else
.BR KF_GIT_MSG .
The kind of change is not distinguished: modified, deleted and renamed read
alike, and an untracked file has no subject to color, so it looks like
anything else that was never committed.
.PP
.B KF_MSG_WIDTH
is a ceiling, not a fixed width. The column is the smallest of three numbers:
that ceiling, the longest subject in the listing, and whatever the terminal
has left once every other field and the longest name have taken their space.
It is therefore never padded past its own text, and never crowds the columns
behind it. Cramped, it stops at
.BR KF_MSG_MIN ;
in a directory where nothing has been committed it vanishes entirely.
.PP
The timestamp changes meaning as well. Under
.B \-G
it is the age of that last commit, phrased as github phrases it \(em
.BR "just now" ,
.BR yesterday ,
.BR "3 weeks ago" ,
.B "2 years ago"
\(em rather than the file's mtime. An entry git knows nothing about falls back
to its mtime in the same phrasing, so the column answers one question
throughout. Without
.B \-G
the timestamp is the mtime, unchanged.
.PP
Outside a work tree
.B \-G
does nothing at all: no empty subject column, and the permissions, link count
and owner it would have displaced stay where they are. The decision is made
per directory, so
.B "kd \-lG repo /tmp"
gives subjects for the one and an ordinary long listing for the other. An
explicit
.B \-\-short
is still honored either way.
.PP
The subjects come from a single
.BR "git log" ,
stopped as soon as every entry has an answer. A directory whose entries were
all touched recently costs almost nothing; one holding a file untouched for
thousands of commits makes the walk go that far back.
.SH SUMMARY ROW
Long format ends with a summary line: the total size of the listed entries
under the size column, and the current time under the timestamp column.
.PP
The total is the sum of
.B st_size
over the listed entries. It is not disk allocation and does not descend into
subdirectories.
.SH PAGER
Long format pages itself when the listing does not fit on screen. A spinning
character at the right edge of the prompt marks that
.B kd
made that decision rather than the user.
.B \-m
forces paging,
.B \-\-no\-pager
disables it.
.PP
The pager is built in. No external program is used, and color is preserved,
which is not the case when piping to
.BR more (1)
or
.BR less (1).
.PP
Keys:
.B q
quits,
.B Enter
advances one line, anything else advances one screen.
.SH SYMLINKS
Link targets are shown only in long format. In other modes a trailing
.B @
marks a link, which remains visible when output is piped and color is off.
Unreachable targets are colored differently from reachable ones.
.SH COLORS
Colors are compiled in, defined as macros in
.IR kf.h .
Each macro holds the raw SGR parameter, so
.B "34"
is blue and an empty string means no color. Editing the header and rebuilding
is the intended way to change the palette.
.PP
Colors stay within the base ANSI range, which resolves through the terminal
palette, so the active theme determines the actual shades. Permissions are
colored per character and the timestamp is split into day, month and time.
.PP
Regular files with no other distinguishing trait are colored by extension.
Extensions are grouped into families \(em archives, images, video, audio,
documents, source code, structured data, markup, keys and certificates, and
build or backup leftovers \(em each with one macro in
.IR kf.h ,
so a whole family retints in a single edit. The
.B KF_EXTENSIONS
table maps extensions to families and is matched without regard to case.
File type and the executable bit take precedence, so an executable
.B .sh
is colored as an executable.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B LS_COLORS
Consulted for file name colors only when
.B \-\-ls\-colors
is given, and it takes precedence over the built-in extension table where it
has a matching entry. Metadata columns always use the compiled-in palette.
.TP
.B LC_COLLATE
Determines sort order where the C library supports collation. A musl build
sorts by code point, as musl has no collation tables.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
An argument was not understood, or a path could not be read.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR ls (1),
.BR eza (1),
.BR tree (1)
.SH AUTHORS
